menu "Lua scripting"

config LUA_ENABLE
    bool "Lua runtime"
    default n
    help
      NOT IMPLEMENTED YET (phase 6). Device protocols are meant to live in Lua
      on LittleFS so they can be changed without a program/erase cycle; only
      the stable pieces (crypto, checksums, TLV primitives) stay in C.

config LUA_HEAP_KB
    int "Lua arena (KB)"
    depends on LUA_ENABLE
    default 128
    help
      A dedicated static arena, not the system heap, so a runaway script hits
      its own ceiling with an ordinary Lua out-of-memory error instead of
      starving lwIP or BTstack. Static so the link-time RAM check can see it.

      Raised from 96 after measurement, not guesswork. COMPILING a module
      costs far more than running it: the 28 KB A2687 profile peaks at 82 KB
      of arena while the parser works, against about 14 KB once it is
      resident. At 96 KB that loaded only into a completely empty arena and
      failed with "not enough memory" the moment any other script was also
      loaded -- which is the normal state of a board being worked on.

      The cost is bss, which this part has to spare. If a future profile
      approaches this again, the answer is to split it into modules rather
      than to keep raising the ceiling: peak cost tracks the size of the
      largest single chunk being compiled, not the total.

config LUA_HOOK_BUDGET
    int "Instructions per scheduler slice before a script is forced to yield"
    depends on LUA_ENABLE
    default 20000

endmenu
